airs a sprite with its destination control, while the L-bend   gives the orientation-independent anchor cell to place on that destination. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "marked L-shaped sprite s with one embedded marker pixel",   "dst": "isolated control pixel d",   "match_cond": "s.marker.color == d.color and d lies on the outer diagonal ray from s.L_bend through s.marker",   "transform": "translate s so s.L_bend lands on d; clear original s and consumed d; keep unmatched sprites and unrelated single pixels unchanged" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="8" source="train" puzzle_id="e9fc42f2"> <similarity> score=1.0627 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0136 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/e9fc42f2.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/e9fc42f2.json </paths> <description> Assemble the foreground objects by docking equal-colored boundary markers edge-to-edge.  Input structure The grid has a dominant background color and several 4-connected multicolor foreground objects.  One foreground color is the body color; the less frequent foreground colors are boundary connector markers, and each marker color appears once as a free end or twice as a matched docking pair.  Rule Crop each foreground object to its bounding box and translate the objects so that the two occurrences of every paired connector color touch on their exposed boundary sides.  Preserve each object's orientation and colors, then return the tight crop around the assembled foreground on the original background color.  Key insight The colored marker pixels are ports: matching colors specify which objects must touch, and the marker's side of its object determines the direction of the translation. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "4-connected foreground object sprite s with boundary connector markers",   "dst": "matching boundary connector marker d