 color-matched singleton markers.  Input structure: - Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers). - Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.  Rule: - Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color. - Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position. - Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.  Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "sprite s with anchor pixel inside the source framed rectangle",   "dst": "anchored on singleton marker pixel d inside the destination framed rectangle",   "match_cond": "s.anchor_color == d.color",   "transform": "overlap s.anchor with d",   "grid_shape": "crop to only retain the destination rectangle" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="7" source="train" puzzle_id="54dc2872"> <similarity> score=1.0618 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0090 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/54dc2872.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/54dc2872.json </paths> <description> Move each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.  Input structure - The background is the dominant color. - Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls. - Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker   pixel of a different color.  Rule - If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,   translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel. - Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,   then paste all translated sprites; sprites without a matching control stay put.  Key insight - The marker color pairs a sprite with its