from each panel; when a later object overlaps earlier objects, slide it by the nearest in-bounds non-overlapping offset",   "grid_shape": "5x17 input with separator columns -> 5x5 output" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="5" source="train" puzzle_id="df978a02"> <similarity> score=1.0533 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0092 code_feature=0.4286 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/df978a02.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/df978a02.json </paths> <description> Let the largest center-pointing object grow outward while every other object loses its inward tip.  Input structure - The grid has a single dominant background color and four 8-connected monochromatic foreground objects. - The objects surround a central background gap and point toward it from cardinal directions. - Exactly one object has the largest area.  Rule - Find the central background gap by minimizing aggregate distance to the four objects. - Recolor the nearest-to-center pixel of each non-largest object to background. - Add a three-pixel cap in the largest object's color on the side away from the center, centered on the shared row or column.  Key insight - The edit is a transfer of prominence around a common center: losers retract at their tips, and the unique largest object extends outward from the center. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "transform_in_place",   "src": "four 8-connected monochromatic objects pointing toward a shared central bg pixel",   "transform": "identify the unique largest object; recolor each other object's nearest-to-center tip pixel to bg and add a 3-pixel cap in the largest object's color on its side away from the center" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="6" source="eval" puzzle_id="a251c730"> <similarity> score=1.0640 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0199 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/eval/a251c730.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/a251c730.json </paths> <description> Stamp source sprites into a destination frame via color-matched singleton markers.  Input structure: - Two bordered rectangles: source (multi-cell sprites) and destination (singleton markers). - Each sprite has an anchor pixel — its least-frequent color. Markers share these colors.  Rule: - Match each destination marker to the source sprite with the same anchor color. - Translate the sprite so its anchor overlaps the marker position. - Output is the destination rectangle, interior cleared before stamping.  Key insight: The rarest color per sprite doubles as a template key and positioning anchor. </description> <annotation> {   "type": "move_to_match",   "src": "sprite s with anchor pixel inside the source framed rectangle",   "dst": "anchored on singleton marker pixel d inside the destination framed rectangle",   "match_cond": "s.anchor_color == d.color",   "transform": "overlap s.anchor with d",   "grid_shape": "crop to only retain the destination rectangle" } </annotation> </reference_puzzle>  <reference_puzzle rank="7" source="train" puzzle_id="54dc2872"> <similarity> score=1.0618 mechanism=1.0000 lexical=0.0089 code_feature=0.5000 overlap_tags=none </similarity> <paths> code=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/ARC_code/train/54dc2872.py annotation=/home/lixinyu/snap/new/annotations/54dc2872.json </paths> <description> Move each marked L-shaped sprite onto the separate pixel matching its marker color.  Input structure - The background is the dominant color. - Foreground has 4-connected multicolor sprites and isolated one-pixel controls. - Each sprite has one repeated body color in an L shape plus one embedded marker   pixel of a different color.  Rule - If the embedded marker color also appears as a separate isolated control pixel,   translate the whole sprite so the body L-bend lands on that control pixel. - Clear each moved sprite from its old position, clear its consumed control pixel,   then